  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-277275",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=\\sqrt{6x-5},$ we replace $x$ with $6x-5$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n6x-5\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-5 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 6x & \\ge 5 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [\\frac{5}{6},\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14239347/assets/4d0a6205a61aa0625a082d6d3bc1941ab00e0536c85844f28af873798bf6db64.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
